#!/bin/sh
# Git post-checkout hook — restamps the build number after HEAD moves WITHOUT a commit.
#
# ─── THE GAP THIS CLOSES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# post-commit stamps AFTER the commit is written, so the commit's own project.pbxproj
# still carries the PREVIOUS count. That is harmless while you build from the tip —
# the working tree was restamped a moment later. It is NOT harmless the moment you
# check something out:
#
#     git checkout stable-91   ->   working tree says CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION = 90
#
# The rollback build then calls itself 90 and there are now two different binaries
# claiming to be build 90. That is precisely the confusion the standard exists to end:
# "a working iPad and three broken iPhones could not be told apart."
#
# Caught by hand on 2026-09-07 (the stable-91 tag committed 90) and stamped manually
# that night. A thing caught by hand is a thing that will be missed by hand.
#   -> feedback_dont_spend_reliability_on_what_machinery_can_do
#
# ─── WHY post-checkout AND NOT A BUILD PHASE ────────────────────────────────────
# Same reason stamp-build.sh gives: ENABLE_USER_SCRIPT_SANDBOXING = YES means a Run
# Script phase cannot read .git. The stamp is written before the build, so every way
# HEAD can move needs a hook. post-commit covers committing; this covers checking out.
#
# ─── $3 IS NOT OPTIONAL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Git calls this hook for FILE checkouts too (`git checkout -- SomeFile.swift`), where
# HEAD has not moved at all. The third argument is 1 for a branch/tag checkout and 0
# for a file checkout. Without this test the hook would rewrite project.pbxproj every
# time a single file is restored.
set -e
[ "$3" = "1" ] || exit 0
GIT=/usr/bin/git
ROOT=$($GIT rev-parse --show-toplevel) || exit 0
[ -x "$ROOT/Scripts/stamp-build.sh" ] || exit 0
exec /bin/sh "$ROOT/Scripts/stamp-build.sh" >/dev/null 2>&1
